This is a manual that explains files with .gameobj extension.

Lines that start with # mean comments.

Lines that start with M or m indicate some meta information like object name, object size, etc.
The meta information in written in that way:
m <type> <value>
For example:
m name house1

WARNING! between every chunk of data describing one thing must be only ONE space but they must be on the same line,  and
     chunks of data describing different things must be at different lines.

Below are presented all types of meta information and their expected value:
    name -> string with object name (same as file name without extension)
    width -> int number with width of object in game blocks. Required field.
    height -> int number with height of object in game blocks. Required field.
    clearArea -> true or false. If value is set to true then all blocks that are in area of object but not mentioned in file would be set to null.
        If value is set to false those blocks would be left. Default value is false.



Then you have to specify blocks, in such way:
b <mesh type> <x> <y>
where:
    <mesh type> -> stands for string that is a key for certain mesh in Map<String, Mesh[]> meshMap in MapManager class
    <x> -> int var, representing block position along x axis relative to top left corner of object
    <y> -> int var, representing block position along y axis relative to top left corner of object

For example:
b dirt 0 0
stands for dirt block in top left corner of object



If you want to specify same blocks one after another, you can specify area where to put that blocks.
Area of blocks is specified in that way:
ba <mesh type> <x1> <y1> <x2> <y1>
where:
    <x1> -> start x index of area
    <y1> -> start y index of area
    <x2> -> end x index of area. Must be greater or equal to <x1>
    <y2> -> end y index of area. Must be greater or equal to <y1>

For example:
ba dirt 0 0 2 0
will create stip of length 3 along x axis made by dirt blocks.


You can also optionally specify rotation along x,y and z axes as the last parameter.
For example:
b wood_desk_triangle 0 0 0,0,180
Possible values for rotation are: 0, 90, 180, 270.
You cant and any spaces between rotation values and you must specify all of them even if one or two of them are 0;
You can also add rotation for area description, then rotation will be applied for every block in the area.



To add wall you just need to properly specify mesh type and if it starts with "wall_" is will be automatically considered as wall.
Area specification and rotation can be also applied on walls.


To add structures you have to follow this pattern:
s <mesh type> <z index> <can move throw> <x> <y>
where:
    <mesh type> -> just mesh name
    <z index> -> z index of structure positons. Can be specified as absolute value(for example 2.5). Or relative to block/wall z index (and more).
        For z index specification you and use numbers with(or without) decimal part (don`t add "f" to numbers), operators: "+" "-" "*" "/", and
        some special keys which are presented below:
            "z_block" -> z index of block
            "z_wall" -> z index of wall
            "block_length" -> length of block
        You can`t add any spaces inside block of operations but you use parentheses.
    <can move throw> -> true or false
    <x> -> start x index of structure
    <y> -> start y index of structure

For example:
s struct_door z_block-block_length/2+0.01 false 0 0

You can`t specify area for structures but you can specify rotation as always.
Every block or wall can be defined as structure.